course: adult-life
lesson: 42
title: Our street: feuds, newcomers and stories that grow
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A story passed from person to person is like a snowball rolling downhill: it picks up bits on the way. «Debbie's visiting her sister in Australia» goes past three people and comes back as «Debbie's selling up and going for good».
explain: Stories grow because people fill gaps with guesses, round numbers up, and add a twist that makes the tale better to tell. One clue plus a guess plus another guess feels like news, but it is not.
explain: Scene: in Dublin, Nan sees a man with a tape measure outside the Kellys' house. By teatime she says: «They're selling up, sign going up any day, they've been arguing for months.» The tape measure was real. The sign, the selling and the arguing were all added on the way.
explain: Some exaggeration is just fun when everyone is in on the joke — «the Coopers' Christmas lights can be seen from space». But when a real name is added to a crime, or a guess is made about someone's marriage, money or mind, the story can follow that person for years.
explain: The best checks are simple: read the sign yourself, ask the person kindly, or say «I don't know» — it is allowed.
fact: A story told through three people often comes back a different shape; a visit can become an emigration.
fact: A joke label like «midlife crisis» is not a fact about someone's mind; admiring a car is fine, diagnosing a person is unkind.
fact: Exaggeration that everyone knows is a joke, like lights «seen from space», is harmless fun.
fact: Ghost stories and playground legends are fun, but they should not turn a real neighbour into a villain.
fact: If someone stops coming to something, ask how they are instead of deciding they have fallen out with you.
fact: Families don't owe the street their news first; when a baby arrives, the kind reply is «congratulations».
fact: A learner driver has L plates and a grown-up beside them; check before you accuse anyone of breaking the law.
fact: Liking or disliking a purple door is an opinion, not news; sneering at people for it is unkind.
fact: Never put a real person's name onto a crime story; the police name people when they have reason to, not the playground.
fact: Being glad for someone's success without hunting for a catch is part of being a good neighbour.
fact: Bad-news gossip can feel caring but still spreads fear; offer help, not headlines.
fact: Small weddings are a choice many people make because they are cheaper and calmer, not a sign of a family row.
fact: «Where do they get the money?» is usually envy dressed up as a question.
fact: People change jobs and move all the time; not every leaving hides a secret.
exercise: Mum: «Debbie from the Co-op's moving to Australia for good, so I heard.» What did Debbie actually say? | She's visiting her sister in Australia | She's selling her house this month to a young couple | She's leaving her job at the Co-op | She's moving abroad with her family
exercise: Nan: «Mr Ali's got a red sports car. Midlife crisis, that is.» What's the fact and what's the label? | The red car is fact; the crisis is a joke label | Both are facts that Nan has checked with the people involved | The crisis is fact; the car is a joke | Neither is a fact, it's all made up
exercise: Dad: «Coopers' lights can be seen from space, bill must be a grand!» Is this harmful gossip? | No, it's fun exaggeration everyone gets | Yes, it's spreading lies about their money | Yes, the Coopers will be very upset | No, because it's all completely true
exercise: Kids: «The Grants' garage is haunted, Mr Grant came out with WHITE HAIR!» What's really true? | The light flickers and he has grey hair | A ghost lives in the Grants' garage | Mr Grant's hair turned white in a night | Mr Grant keeps something scary in there
exercise: Auntie Bev: «Linda's missed four coffee mornings, she's fallen out with us all.» What's the kind thing to do? | Ask Linda how she's doing | Stop inviting Linda altogether | Ask everyone else what Linda said | Wait for Linda to say sorry first
exercise: Mum: «Turners had a baby and NOBODY knew she was pregnant!» What does «nobody knew» really mean here? | Mum didn't know; family and friends did | The mother herself didn't know she was having a baby | Not a single person was told | The hospital kept it a secret
exercise: Grandad: «That lad from 19 is driving his dad's car, he's not old enough!» What might be true instead? | He's seventeen, learning with his dad | He has stolen his dad's car keys | He is far too young to sit behind a wheel | He drives alone every night of the week
exercise: Dad: «Number 28 painted their door PURPLE. Some people have no taste.» What is «no taste» here? | An opinion, not news | A fact about Number 28 | A rule broken by Number 28 | A complaint for the council
exercise: Nan: «Kellys are selling up, mark my words, they've been arguing for months.» What did she actually see? | A man measuring the outside of the house | A for-sale sign in their front garden | The Kellys shouting in the street | A removal van at their front door
exercise: Big brother: «The bloke at 14 nicked the lead off the church roof!» What's true? | Lead was stolen; the police named nobody | The man at 14 was arrested for it last month and charged | The police found the lead in his van | The vicar saw him on the church roof
exercise: Mum: «O'Brien twins both got into the grammar — must've had a tutor every day for years.» What's the problem with the tutor bit? | It's a guess that makes their success seem less | It's true, and the twins should own up and say so | It's fine because tutors cost money | It shows the test must have been very easy that year
exercise: Auntie Jen: «Steve's been made redundant, he'll lose the house!» What did Steve really say? | He's looking for a new job | He has lost his house to the bank | He's moving out of town for good | He was sacked for doing wrong
exercise: Kids: «Number 8 has a snake that EATS RABBITS and it escaped!» What's the truth? | They keep a small corn snake in a tank | The snake ate a neighbour's pet rabbit in one go | The snake is loose in the gardens | The snake is big and dangerous
exercise: Nan: «Carol's girl married in a registry office, six people! Must've fallen out with everyone.» What's fairer? | Many people choose small, calm weddings | Six guests always means there has been a family row | They couldn't afford to invite family | Nobody wanted to go to her wedding
exercise: Mrs Okafor: «Greyhound man talks to his dogs like people. Must be lonely.» What's true about this? | Lots of happy people chat to their dogs | Only lonely people talk to their dogs | He must have no friends or family | He should see a doctor about it
exercise: Dad: «Websters cut down that old tree, no warning!» What did really happen? | They told the next-door neighbours first | They did it in secret in the night | They broke the law by cutting it | They did it to annoy the street
exercise: Kids: «Mrs Ford was a SPY, she speaks six languages!» What's true? | She was a civil servant and speaks French and German | She was a secret agent for the government in the war years | She speaks six languages perfectly | She still works as a spy today
exercise: Mum: «The chippy's closing down, rent's gone up!» Where did the story come from? | A sign saying «closed for refurbishment» | The owners told everyone they were broke | The landlord put up a notice in the window | A letter from the council about the rent
test: Auntie Bev: «New woman at 23's a famous actress, apparently, was on telly in the nineties.» What's the real evidence? | Someone thought she looked familiar | She showed them old photos from telly | Her name is on the credits of a show | She signs autographs at the shop
test: Grandad: «Murphys never cut their grass, you could lose a sheep in there.» What might the real reason be? | A bad back, or leaving it for the bees | They're lazy and don't care about anyone | They want to annoy the whole street | They've moved out and left the house
test: Nan: «Bakers in Spain AGAIN, three hols this year, where do they get the money?» One of the «hols» was a weekend at Grandma's. What's really behind Nan's question? | A bit of envy, dressed up as a question | A real worry about the Bakers' bills | A plan to go with them next time | Proof that the Bakers are hiding money
test: Auntie Jen: «Vicar's leaving! Something must've happened, they don't just leave.» What did the vicar actually do? | Announce in church she's moving parish | Leave in the night with no goodbye | Get sent away by the bishop for something | Refuse to say where she's going
test: Kids at the park: «Jamie's dad has a Lamborghini, a yellow one, I saw it!» It was hired for one birthday afternoon. Who could this rumour make awkward? | Jamie, if friends expect him to be rich | Jamie's dad, who has to give the car back to the garage | The kid who saw it, for telling | Nobody, sports cars are just fun
test: Your mate: «Mum said Debbie said she's off for good. No wait — my auntie said it.» How many people did the story go through? | Several, so it may have changed shape | Just one, so it must be right | None, it's what Debbie said herself | Two, so it's guaranteed true now
test: Big brother: «Lad at 14 nicked the lead, everyone at school says so.» What's the right reply? | Don't put his name on it, the police named nobody | If everyone says it, it must be true, so it is fine to say it | Tell the vicar so she can thank the school | Ask the police to arrest him today
test: Sister: «I'm gonna tell everyone the Grants' garage is haunted, it's hilarious.» Why think twice? | Mr Grant is a real neighbour, kids scream at him | It might make a real ghost angry | The garage might really be haunted by an old owner | The Grants might sell up and leave
test: Nan says «midlife crisis» about Mr Ali's car, and he overhears. What does it do to him? | It might hurt or embarrass him | Nothing, it's just about his car | It makes him want a bigger car | It's a compliment about his age
test: Dad: «I'd never chop down a tree like that without asking!» Later: the roots were cracking the drains. What should people have done? | Asked what happened before blaming them | Reported the Websters to the police for cutting it | Planted a new tree in their garden | Never spoken to the Websters again
test: Mum reads the chippy sign: «Closed for refurbishment, back on the 14th.» What should she do with her story? | Correct it, it's only shut for two weeks | Keep it going, the rent will go up anyway | Tell people it's closing on the 14th | Say she heard it from someone else
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the process whereby «visiting a sister» becomes «emigrating permanently»? | A narrative is progressively altered in successive retellings | A narrative is faithfully preserved in successive retellings | The original speaker has deliberately misled her audience | The listeners have consulted an official record of emigration
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the name of a resident ought not to be attached to an unsolved theft. | An innocent person may thereby acquire an undeserved reputation | The police are obliged to publish all names within one week | Thefts from churches are not considered criminal offences | Residents are prohibited from discussing local events
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the young driver at Number 19. | He may lawfully be learning to drive under supervision | He is incontrovertibly below the minimum driving age | He has been reported to the authorities and charged | He has been prohibited from entering the vehicle
exam: It is given that a neighbour has been absent from four consecutive social gatherings. Determine the most appropriate conduct. | To enquire courteously after her wellbeing | To conclude that she has quarrelled with all present | To cease forthwith to extend any invitation to her | To solicit the opinions of others as to her motives
